{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578684564,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMzNTk2NzU5Mw=="},"target":"eb2e719100fa65b8930e57f35d7f7d88c0ec8041c61cc81e57dfdc4bcd97f914","message":"Aspect of original #1956 reported by @mvdoc on behalf of @yarikoptic \n\nIf a subdataset is found to be available as a subdataset underneath a superdataset's clone/remote, and ends up being installed from that location, its origin remote should match the one of the superdataset's remote.\n\nImplementing such a behavior will counteract the fix for https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3926 in #3966","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578643298,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5Mzc2MjQwNTc="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1578667880,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3MzA2NTQ5Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4005#issuecomment-573065497"},"message":"\u003e on behalf of yoh\n\nYou just confused some poor user with no connection to datalad :]\n\n---\n\nI think this issue boils down to the same issue @dorianps raised in gh-3968.  As suggested by my [comment][0] there, I'm not sure this a good direction to go, but it seems it has come up enough that perhaps I'm missing something.\n\n[0]: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3968#issuecomment-569822527","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1578669175,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3MzA3MzcwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4005#issuecomment-573073701"},"message":"Pardon if I am out of line here, but I don't understand the conceptual problem with having remotes in subdatasets match the remote in the parent dataset where it came from. This strategy looks more sensible to me than leaving [origin] remotes around when the user never intended to create [origin].\n\nIn terms of use cases, we can imagine a master dataset and multiple researchers working on different [remotes] to produce new data. For example, John works on applying fmri pipelines to produce new subdatasets in his fmri parent dataset, while Brian works to  produce structural mri morphometric data. At the end, the master location needs to be updated with these two new sources of data. They cannot be both [origin] in subdatasets as they are conceptually different sources. Thus, when pulling John's changes it should be as simple as `datalad update --merge --sibling john \u0026\u0026 datalad get .` and the same for `--sibling brian`.\n\nI find collaborative data processing to be the strength of Datalad, so I would facilitate this scenario and avoid having [origin] when is neither needed nor requested by the user... even this means the logic of remotes changes from git.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1578669175,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMzNTgyMzc5Mg=="},"target":"a2d2557a8ee9513f0c29730604a4d79a32acda6d3ebb002d92aaee22d03c4904","message":"Pardon if I am out of line here, but I don't understand the conceptual problem with having remotes in subdatasets match the remote in the parent dataset where it came from. This strategy looks more sensible to me than leaving [origin] remotes around when the user never intended to create [origin].\n\nIn terms of use cases, we can imagine a master dataset and multiple researchers working on different [remotes] to produce new data. For example, John works on applying fmri pipelines to produce new subdatasets in his fmri parent dataset, while Brian works to  produce structural mri morphometric data. At the end, the master location needs to be updated with these two new sources of data. They cannot be both [origin] in subdatasets as they are conceptually different sources. Thus, when pulling John's changes it should be as simple as `datalad update --merge --sibling john \u0026\u0026 datalad get .` and the same for `--sibling brian`.\n\nI find collaborative data processing to be the strength of Datalad, so I would facilitate this scenario and avoid having [origin] when is neither needed nor requested by the user... even if this means the logic of remotes is broken from what git prefers to do.","files":null}]}